A1. NORMALIZATION, ACTUAL KERNEL AND RECIPROCAL PERRON ROOT: PASS
The base measure is uniform probability on a finite cube, L>=1. P=P_rho has positive entries and positive Walsh eigenvalues; Q=P_sqrt(rho) is invertible with Q^2=P. Normalizing h>0 by E hPh=1 loses no associated chain or normalized density. g=Ph, pi=hg, u=h/g, N=Eu and r=u/N are well-defined; both pi and r integrate to one. a=Eh lies in(0,1] by the constant component of Qh.
For K_xy=P_xy h_y/g_x the row sum is1, and nu_x K_xy=mu_x P_xy h_x h_y=W_xy is symmetric. Multiplication by sqrt(pi) gives the stated similarity between K and A_u=M_sqrt(u) P M_sqrt(u): diag(sqrt(pi))^-1 A_u diag(sqrt(pi))=K. A_u sqrt(pi)=sqrt(pi), so strict positivity identifies its simple Perron root as1; PSD gives nonnegative spectrum. T_r=A_u/N has Perron root1/N and exactly the same normalized gap as K. None of this identifies T_pi with K.
For X=M_sqrt(u)Q, XX*=A_u and X*X=QM_uQ have identical finite spectra. The latter PSD operator has norm1; its unit-constant Rayleigh quotient is N, hence N<=1. This is the upper-bound direction needed in the theorem. Under pi probability, z=1/g has E_pi z=a and E_pi z^2=N, so Cauchy gives a^2<=N. Therefore the Perron conclusion is1<=lambda1(T_r)<=1/a^2. No r moment or stationary floor was inserted.
The b boundaries in the model are consistent: b>1 is infeasible, b=1 forces pi=1 and then h=1 by the finite scaling uniqueness proved in this manuscript. Fixed rho,b,C and fixed finite p/B are the relevant quantifiers. No endpoint-uniform assertion is made.

