Exact pi matching gives E pi Pi_<=k g=Eg=a. Holder and the tail bound give E pi g<=a+epsilon sqrt(v). Cauchy under the finite measure h dmu instead gives1=(Ehg)^2<=a Epi g. Subtracting a from the resulting1/a upper comparison yields v/a<=epsilon sqrt(v). At v>0 this gives sqrt(v)<=a epsilon and the stated bounds on a^2 and v; at v=0 no division is needed.
The identity ||Qh-1||2^2=2(1-a), together with2[1-(1+x)^-1/2]<=x for x>=0, yields the displayed epsilon norm bound. Applying Q once more gives ||g-1||p'<=epsilon by the same contractivity parameters. At k=L the actual tail is zero, so v=0 and invertibility forces h=1. The numerical epsilon need not vanish for this endpoint for the inequalities to remain valid.
At s=(1+rho)/(1-rho), s>1+rho and rho/(s-1)=(1-rho)/2, giving exactly the critical B and eta. k is the integer order of degree matching; see the minor clarity observation below. Exponential convergence requires fixed eta<1 and bounded B, as supplied by the intended fixed-parameter setting.

