A4. FINITE SCALING, COERCIVITY, INTEGER BLOCKS AND ACTUAL REALIZATION: PASS
The reversible positive-diagonal scaling lemma is proved rather than imported by label. R_xy=mu_x P_xy is symmetric, w_x=mu_x pi_x is positive and sums to1. Differentiating J=.5 sum R_xy exp(z_x+z_y)-sum w_x z_x yields gradient mu_x[e^z_x(Pe^z)_x-pi_x], including the correct diagonal factor. The Hessian directional form is .5 sum R_xy exp(z_x+z_y)(v_x+v_y)^2. A nonzero direction has a strictly positive diagonal contribution, so strict convexity has no additive-constant gauge.
Writing M=max z, m=min z, c0=.5 min R_xx>0 and w*=min w_x>0, the two lower bounds J>=c0 exp(2M)-M and J>=w*(M-m)-M are valid. On J<=A they give M>=-A, M<=M_A, and m>=-A-(A+M_A)/w*. The direction is correct even for negative A; a nonempty sublevel automatically has A+M>=0. Thus the nonempty sublevel through z=0 is compact and yields a global minimizer, unique by strict convexity. Its zero gradient realizes hPh=pi and energy1. The constants may depend on dimension; the proof needs no uniform coercivity. Positive diagonal is sufficient even if some off-diagonal entries vanish, while the Boolean application has all entries positive.
For every L with m=k_L+1<=L the largest positive odd n<=L/m exists and lies in(L/m-2,L/m]. Disjoint m blocks of size n fit, unused coordinates are harmless, and n is asymptotic to sqrt(L)/C. Each majority is balanced and sign-valued. Every nonzero Fourier term in their product uses a nonempty subset of each disjoint block, hence has degree at least m. Thus pi=1+tF has all required labelled marginals, not merely averaged matching. The product is balanced and pi has mean1.
Only finitely many L can have k_L+1>L; pi=h=1 covers these. The same odd-block formula works in every other finite dimension, including a finite irregular prefix and k=0. The manuscript's 'sufficiently large' introduction does not restrict the algebraic definition, though an optional wording clarification is recorded below. Scaling makes these densities actual hPh stationary laws, and the opening K/W identities prove their actual stationarity.

