A10. FULL SUPPORT346 PROOF, EXPLICIT RANGE AND NO MASS-CARDINALITY LEAP: PASS
The main support theorem needs only h>0, energy1, floor and critical cap. It uses no matching,341,334 or345 premise. Let alpha0=2^-L, a0=(1+rho)/2 and d=a0^L. Every P entry is at most its diagonal d; pi_y>=d h_y^2. Therefore I_x(A)<=sqrt(d) sum_A sqrt(pi_y). Finite-sum Holder, not probability Holder with a missing factor, gives sum_A sqrt(pi)<=m^(1-1/(2s))(2/alpha0)^(1/(2s)). This is precisely sqrt(B)m^theta alpha0^-1/(2s).
For the outside sum, h>=b/g and reciprocal weighted Cauchy give O_x>=b P(x,A^c)^2/(P^2h)(x)>=b(1-md)^2/(P^2h)(x). Replacing the outside g-sum by the larger P^2h weakens a lower bound in the correct direction. md<1 ensures a positive outside mass. It is not permissible to use the numerator estimate alone; the proof does not.
Semigroup composition gives P^2h=P_rho^(3/2)(Qh), where this notation means noise parameter rho^(3/2). Its point-evaluation norm squared under uniform L2 is sum_y P_rho^(3/2)(x,y)^2/alpha0=P_rho^3(x,x)/alpha0=(1+rho^3)^L. Thus energy1 gives P^2h(x)<=(1+rho^3)^(L/2). The rho^3 and atom factor are both correct.
Combining I/O yields exactly R=(sqrt(B)/b)m^theta[2^(1/s)a0(1+rho^3)]^(L/2)/(1-md)^2. Since actual K(x,A)=I/(I+O), its bound is R/(1+R). Every killed row sum is at most that value, hence so is its spectral radius. Reversibility makes the killed operator self-adjoint; similarity to a principal submatrix of A_u also gives PSD. Supported signed Rayleigh quotients obey the same bound without pretending that support is invariant under full K. Summing escape over A yields J/nu(A)>=1/(1+R), valid even when nu(A)>1/2.
For kappa<1 and fixed tau strictly below both stated bounds, md and m^theta kappa^(L/2) decay uniformly for integer m<=exp(tau L). At0<rho<=1/4, log kappa<=rho[17/16-(8/5)log2]<-rho/240 using log2>2/3 and16/15-17/16=1/240. With m<=exp(rho L/1920), theta<1 yields the exponent1/1920-1/480=-1/640. Also md<=((5/8)exp(1/7680))^L<=(2/3)^L because log(16/15)>=1/16>1/7680. Thus the denominator inverse square is<=9 for every L>=1. All constants, rounding, included rho=1/4 and singleton small-L cases are valid.
At kappa=1, singleton escape has the stated positive dimension-uniform lower bound. At rho=1/2, kappa=27*2^(1/3)/32>1, verified by cubing positive quantities. This limits the sufficient estimate, not the actual gap. Small stationary mass only implies |A|<=2^L nu(A)/b via the floor; at exp(-c sqrt L) this can greatly exceed exp(rho L/1920). The combined manuscript correctly leaves this uncovered range open and never reverses a cap inequality to remove it.

